# Horizontal rule

@lede
Exactly three dashes on a line by themselves.
@end

## The form

```brief
---
```

A live example, between this paragraph and the next:

---

That is the entire surface. Two dashes is too few; four dashes is too
many — both are @ec[B0304].

## Why exactly three

There are five surface forms in common Markdown that mean "horizontal
rule": `---`, `***`, `___`, `* * *`, `- - -`. Brief picks one (`---`) and
refuses the rest. The rule is consistent with Brief's broader stance: one
canonical spelling per construct.

## When to use it

Sparingly. A horizontal rule means "the topic shifts here in a way that a
heading would not capture." If the next thing is conceptually a new
section, use a heading. If the content above and below is genuinely
unrelated, consider whether they belong in the same document.

## In LLM mode

Emitted as a literal `---` line. LLMs read it as a topic break, the same
way a human reader does.
